1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,600
 most AI projects fail before a single line of code is written and the root cause is almost always

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:13,760
 poor problem selection. Teams rush into building solutions without validating whether they're

3
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:20,320
 solving the right problem or whether AI is even the right tool. When the problem is wrong,

4
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:26,560
 even a technically perfect solution delivers zero value. Common failure patterns repeat across

5
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:33,040
 organizations. AI is often applied where simpler tools like spreadsheets or role-based automation

6
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:39,840
 would work better, creating unnecessary complexity and cost. Many projects lack a clear business

7
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:46,160
 owner, which leads to weak prioritization and no accountability for adoption. Others never

8
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:52,560
 defined success metrics, making it impossible to demonstrate value to leadership. The most

9
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:59,840
 dangerous failure mode is uncontrolled scope growth. What starts as a focused idea slowly expands

10
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:06,480
 into a vague platform, delaying delivery indefinitely. This slide sets the tone for the capstone.

11
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:13,280
 Success begins with restraint, clarity and discipline. Choosing the right problem is not a

12
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:20,160
 preliminary step. It is the most important design decision you will make. Not every inefficiency

13
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:26,880
 deserves an AI solution. Business-worthy problems share specific characteristics that signal

14
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:34,240
 real return on investment. First, the problem must be frequent and repeatable. AI compounds value

15
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:41,680
 over time, so one off issues rarely justify the cost. Second, the inefficiency must be observable.

16
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:48,080
 Teams should be able to point to exactly where time is lost, errors occur, or throughput slows down.

17
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:55,520
 Vague frustration is not enough. There must be a concrete bottleneck. Third, affected stakeholders

18
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:01,360
 must be clearly known. Everyone is not a stakeholder. You should be able to name the roles

19
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:07,840
 experiencing the pain. Fourth, outcomes must matter to the business. Solving the problem should

20
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:14,480
 reduce cost, mitigate risk, improve customer experience, or protect revenue in measurable ways.

21
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:20,560
 Finally, apply the litmus test. If this were an internal product with a price tag,

22
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,200
 would teams willingly pay for it? Real value creates real commitment.

23
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:33,840
 A critical skill in AI product work is knowing which idea is not to build. Many projects fail

24
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:40,240
 because they start with vague or overly ambitious problem statements. A chat bot for everything

25
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:47,200
 sounds exciting, but lacks a defined workflow, user, or success metric. It tries to solve

26
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:54,080
 too much and ends up solving nothing. Similarly, vague productivity assistance fail because they

27
00:02:54,080 --> 00:03:00,480
 don't specify which task is being improved, for whom or how success is measured. Another common

28
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:06,800
 anti-pattern is AI without a user. Projects built because we should use AI, not because someone

29
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:13,600
 needs it. Good problems look very different. Support ticket summarization has a clear input,

30
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:20,240
 output, user, and measurable impact. Internal policy search addresses a known pain point

31
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:26,720
 with a defined corpus and clear success metrics. Analytics Q&A for executives serves a narrow

32
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:34,240
 audience with high value decisions. The lesson is simple, clarity beats ambition. Narrow well-defined

33
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:41,200
 problems are where AI succeeds. If you don't know the primary user, stop. Every successful

34
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:47,600
 AI project is built around a specific role, not a department, not everyone who might benefit.

35
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,640
 The primary user is someone who will interact with the system frequently and whose workflow

36
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:59,280
 you understand deeply. Start by asking who uses this tool daily. Daily users develop habits

37
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:05,200
 and muscle memory. Occasionally users forget how systems work and abandon them. Next,

38
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:10,640
 identify who actually feels the pain. It's often not the manager, but the individual doing the

39
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:16,560
 repetitive work. You must also understand who approves the solution financially or operationally,

40
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:23,200
 and who is accountable if it fails. Someone's metrics, reputation, or workflow must be on the line.

41
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:29,520
 That accountability drives adoption, designing for one clear primary user forces focus.

42
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:36,640
 Generalization can come later, but only after value is proven. A strong core use case is surgical

43
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:44,560
 in scope. One problem, one job, one workflow. It represents the minimum viable slice that delivers

44
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:51,440
 value. Everything else is distraction. If you can't describe the use case in a simple input to

45
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:59,040
 output diagram, it's not ready. Avoid feature creep aggressively. Fraces like while we're at it

46
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:04,800
 are warning signs that delivery timelines are about to slip. Instead, understand what happens

47
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:10,240
 before and after your AI step in the workflow. Your system does not operate in isolation.

48
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:17,760
 Be explicit about what the AI does versus what humans do. Hybrid workflows often outperform

49
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:23,760
 full automation in business contexts. Clarity here prevents unsafe or unrealistic expectations.

50
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:30,560
 A well-defined use case is not limiting. It is what enables successful delivery. One of the most

51
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:37,120
 overlooked aspects of scoping is defining what you will not build. Constraints enable success.

52
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:43,600
 Explicitly documenting non-goals protects your team from endless expansion and misaligned

53
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:50,640
 expectations. List out of scope features clearly. We will not support PDF uploads in version one.

54
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:56,400
 It's far more effective than vague statements. Avoid mixing future roadmap ideas into current

55
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:02,800
 scope. Parking lot features have a habit of sneaking into builds. Another critical boundary is

56
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:09,600
 automation. Define which decisions require human oversight. Fully autonomous systems are rarely

57
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:16,000
 appropriate for business critical workflows. Specify what the AI can recommend versus what it

58
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,160
 can execute. Writing down non-goals sets expectations and builds trust with stakeholders.

59
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:30,240
 It signals maturity and focus, not limitation. If you can't measure success, it doesn't exist.

60
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:37,760
 Metrics convert subjective opinions into objective outcomes and force alignment before development

61
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:45,600
 begins. Start with time-saved per task. Even small per task improvements compound quickly at scale.

62
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:51,760
 Error reduction is another powerful metric, especially in compliance sensitive environments.

63
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:56,800
 Consistency improvements matter for quality and downstream processes.

64
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:03,600
 User adoption is often the most honest signal. If intended users don't voluntarily use the

65
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:10,400
 system, something is wrong. Finally, translate improvements into cost or effort reduction that

66
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:17,280
 leadership understands. Define these metrics upfront, not after deployment. Metrics are not

67
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:24,640
 for justification. They are for direction. A critical distinction in AI projects is understanding

68
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:32,240
 that accuracy does not equal success. A model can be highly accurate and still fail if it doesn't

69
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:39,040
 move business outcomes. Business metrics reflect what executives care about. Time-saved,

70
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:46,000
 cost-reduced, revenue-protected, and risk-mitigated. Operational metrics like latency,

71
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,640
 uptime, and usage ensure the system functions reliably in production. Model metrics,

72
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:59,920
 precision, recall, F1 are important, but incomplete. Human satisfaction metrics and trust

73
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:06,560
 indicators often matter most. If users don't trust the system, technical excellence is irrelevant.

74
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:13,120
 Successful capstone projects prioritize business impact first and treat model metrics

75
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:21,040
 as supporting evidence, not the goal. Success criteria define what done means before development

76
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:29,600
 begins. Without them, projects drift endlessly and launches are delayed. Clear acceptance criteria

77
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:38,160
 must be specific and testable. Define measurable improvement against a baseline. Plan for safe failure.

78
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:45,360
 AI systems will fail, so design fallback pads and escalation mechanisms in advance.

79
00:08:46,320 --> 00:08:52,960
 Human in the loop requirements should be documented early. Finally, ensure stakeholders can

80
00:08:52,960 --> 00:09:00,400
 objectively sign off on readiness. If success can't be evaluated clearly, it will always be debated.

81
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:08,480
 Before building anything, pause. This readiness gate is your final checkpoint. You should be

82
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:15,280
 able to clearly articulate a business-worthy problem, a narrowly-scoped use case, identified

83
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:22,400
 users and stakeholders, defined success metrics, and explicit constraints. If any of these are

84
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:29,120
 missing, you are not ready to build. Investing extra time and planning will save months of wasted

85
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:35,680
 development. The final test is simple. If you can't explain your problem in one sentence,

86
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:46,640
 go back and refine it. Your future self and your capstone will thank you.

